            {
               "text": "### Images from El Salvador Museo\n\nThese images are copyright for El Salvador Museo and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 2</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgBering\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgBering', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">A map showing Indigenous migration routes across the Bering Strait. Photo taken at the Museo Nacional de Antropología en San Salvador, El Salvador (<htcite0>), on <htdate0>.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>The arrival in Abya Yala (America) - In this text, we will explore different studies on the period when the first peoples could have arrived in <i>Abya Yala</i> (America) (<htcite1>)</li><li>Indigenous Abya Yala (America) - In this text, we study the Abya Yala (American) people as a unique family. (<htcite2>)</li><li>Who truly discovered America (<i>Abya Yala</i>)? - While this question may seem straightforward and readily answered in history books, it's essential to recognize the limitations of solely adhering to one worldview. (<htcite3>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 2</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"img1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img1', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Babylonian world map © Trustees of the British Museum (<htcite4>) superimposed on the modern world map © Mapswire (<htcite5>). Sharing under <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)</a> license.</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Understanding Texts: Equality Levels - Some concepts, like the definition of a planet, change over time. Sometimes, objects with slightly different sizes can be considered equal. How can we use numbers to quantify equality? (<htcite6>)</li><li>History: Are we truly equal? - Some comparisons can be made in a simple way with indisputable results—for example, when comparing circles (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle\"></i>) with squares (<i class=\"fa-solid fa-square\"></i>), we can easily determine whether they are equal or not. In the case of so-called races, however, it is not enough to look at skin color or hair type. The issue must be examined at an internal level, and through genetics we find that we are all the same specie. (<htcite7>)</li><li>Classifying equality - In this text, we see that classifying is more than comparing things that are exactly alike. (<htcite8>)</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
